with its incredible looking environments  in an ornate combat system horizon zero  dawn is an easy game to slip back into  the frozen wilds expansion makes a  return visit even more enticing with new  gear and challenges to seek out in the  frozen north with fresh enemies balanced  to fit into the game's latter half  there's also a new storyline which  slightly expands your knowledge of the  past and hints at events to come those  revelations alone aren't terribly  exciting but as an excuse to revisit one  of the best games of the year  this new journey hardly suffers from a  little disappointment Fresnel wilds  primarily takes place in a previously  unforeseen stretch of land that  encompasses roughly 10 to 15 hours of  new side quests and errands snow is a  new to horizon but it's never felt as  pervasive as it does here as you truck  up crunchy inclines pillowy sheets of  white break under foot while flurries of  powder puffs dance in the air and when  the Sun cuts through the atmosphere just  right individual snowflakes take on a  gorgeous pink hue that makes an already  pretty game even prettier this scenic  territory belongs to a tribe known as  the Bannock who have long lived in  isolation from the rest of society but  Nick tribes follow a strict code of  conduct that has more to do with  self-reliance and pride than it does  with justice and order that constraints  therein put pressure on Bannock in  various ways and most of your objectives  in the frozen wilds focuses on helping  individuals overcome their personal  struggles with tradition your primary  task has bigger implications however you  must seek of a Nuke shaman living on the  outskirts who unknowingly holds the key  to a new chunk of historical data and a  new facet of the technological powers  behind the scenes both this quest line  and personality-driven side quests  deliver heaps of dialogue which like  horizons exchanges at large range from  heartfelt discussions to perfunctory  filler but as excuses to clash with new  sparking mechanical beasts every mission  in the frozen wilds feels valuable  though the selection of fresh monsters  is somewhat small encompassing three  distinct additions they are hugely  formidable opponents that require a  considerable amount of effort to defeat  [Applause]  [Music]  there's also a new difficulty tear for  every enemy in the game one step above  corrupted which makes them faster and  stronger than ever and to top it off  there are bellowing towers that heal  nearby enemies almost faster than you  can damage them requiring an extra step  in some battles that ramps up the  tension from the gecko  of the few new additions the two grizzly  bear enemies one who uses ice and the  other fire stand out from the pack they  are capable of running on all fours or  standing tall on their hind legs and  employ a wide range of hard-hitting  attacks that punish delayed thinking in  a heartbeat  taking them down calls upon expert  targeting and intelligent use of  elements the latter of which is linked  to the new casting staffs introduced  with the pannier I adjusted the chilled  water flow rebalance here strapped on a  launcher and now she shoots freezing  spikes you weren't kidding these purely  elemental weapons are perfect for  exploiting enemy weaknesses to put them  into a vulnerable state to be followed  up by attacks from more traditional  weaponry he's proved useful beyond the  frozen north as well and it's easy to  imagine how their capabilities would  have come in handy during past trials  you'll also find a new grade of armor  that adds an additional modification  slot to standard armor types as well as  the binocs new gear which carries Auto  healing properties most new gear if not  given to you is acquired through the  usual mix of resources which now  includes the territory exclusive  currency blue gleam these are rewarded  for completing quests and exploring the  wilderness and are in very short supply  you have to work to earn your new toys  but that's okay the work is what makes  horizons so enjoyable the biggest and  farthest-reaching addition has to be the  new traveler skill tree geared towards  making your life easier and more  efficient traveler skills include things  like disassembling extra items procuring  resources while on horseback and  expanding your resource - at large all  things would have been immensely useful  throughout horizon seeing these new  skills having already finished the main  game isn't terribly inspiring unless  you've got a lot of lingering quests  left to hunt or if you've been holding  off on starting a new game plus that  said coming back to horizon for the  Frozen Wiles alone is still worthwhile  for the fights and sights but it  ultimately feels like a missing chapter  rather than an eye-opening extension of  what came before it's easy to imagine  how newcomers to horizon will benefit  from its new gear and skills the most  likewise its story feels better suited  as an interlude than the revelatory  companion to the conclusion it tries to  be if these are feelings that come up  after more than a dozen hours of  riveting battles and serene hikes flew  by so it's hard to get too upset at such  a captivating experience when it's all  said and done  [Music]  [Music]
 

